open the menu in a nes or snes game

Tapping MENU in a NES or SNES game froze the handheld, power button and all.
quick_value asks the running console's own row for Shader and Colour
Correction, and playing_row only has one for the Game Boy family and the
GBA; for a console it hands the row straight back, and quick_value asked
again until the stack ran out. The rows are hidden on those consoles, but
the menu still reads every row's value when it draws.

A row that maps to itself now has no value. Found by a test that seats a
real Super Mario World on Snes9x 2005 and taps MENU, which overflowed the
stack on the first menu frame; the test below is the core-free version.

Applies on top of 0029, upstream 91499a9.

diff --git a/crates/slot/src/app.rs b/crates/slot/src/app.rs
--- a/crates/slot/src/app.rs
+++ b/crates/slot/src/app.rs
@@ -964,7 +964,12 @@
             QuickRow::Shader
             | QuickRow::ColourCorrection
             | QuickRow::PlayBrightness
-            | QuickRow::PlayBlueLight => self.quick_value(self.playing_row(row)),
+            // A NES or SNES game has no shader or colour correction of its own,
+            // so the row maps to itself; asking again would never return.
+            | QuickRow::PlayBlueLight => match self.playing_row(row) {
+                same if same == row => None,
+                mapped => self.quick_value(mapped),
+            },
             QuickRow::Brightness => Some(QuickValue::level(self.state.brightness)),
             QuickRow::BlueLight => Some(match self.state.blue_light {
                 0 => QuickValue::Off,
